From cfdf94895ca3d1f04b02150e5b0d7c86eb42f1b9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z3thon <rosson.long@lztek.io>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 13:40:38 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] fix(linux): put child webviews in a per-window gtk::Fixed

With `unstable`, every child webview was packed into the window's vertical
`gtk::Box`. A box stacks its children and ignores positions, so
`Window::add_child(position, size)` and `Webview::set_bounds` did nothing
and two webviews split the window between them (#10420, #13071, #15656,
#16132).

Built as suggested in the review of #15704: one container per window,
created with the window (`create_window` and `CreateRawWindow`, `unstable`
only), packed into `default_vbox` under any menu bar, stored on
`WindowWrapper` and handed to `create_webview` through
`CreateWebviewOptions`. `WindowChild` webviews are built into it, and
`Reparent` moves a webview into the new window's container.

The container is a `gtk::Fixed` subclass that requests no size of its own
(`src/child_container.rs`). A plain `gtk::Fixed` requests its children's
bounding box, which GTK turns into the window's minimum size: with a
webview filling the window, the window could never be made smaller.

Needs wry's gtk::Fixed `set_bounds`/`bounds`/`reparent` fix (based on
tauri-apps/wry#1745) for positions to survive layout passes and size-only
updates.

Tested on Ubuntu 24.04 (WebKitGTK 2.52, X11 under Xvfb): a child lands at
its rect, keeps it across a size-only update and a window relayout, and
the window shrinks below its children; with a plain `gtk::Fixed` the same
harness shows the window stuck at its children's size.
